## The Bug

The problem is that your goroutines capture the loop variable `i` **by reference**. In Go 1.21, the `for` loop uses a single variable `i` that is reused across all iterations. By the time any of the goroutines actually get scheduled and execute, the loop has already finished and `i` has been incremented to `5`. All five goroutines read that same final value.

The relevant lines:

```go
for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
    ...
    go func() {
        ...
        fmt.Println(i)  // captures the shared `i` by reference
    }()
}
```

## The Fix

Pass `i` as an argument to the goroutine's function. This creates a fresh local parameter for each goroutine, snapshotting the value at the time the goroutine is spawned:

<solution language="go" verdict="fix">
package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "sync"
)

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    for i := 0; i < 5; i++ {
        wg.Add(1)
        go func(i int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            fmt.Println(i)
        }(i)
    }
    wg.Wait()
}
</solution>

Each invocation `func(i int){...}(i)` binds the current value of `i` into a new parameter, so every goroutine sees its own copy. (Note: if you ever upgrade to Go 1.22+, the loop variable is already scoped per-iteration, so the original code would work — but since you're on 1.21, the parameter trick is the right fix.)

