## Bug Identification

The crash is on **line 2**:

```javascript
const city = order.shipping_address.city;
```

The error `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'city')` means `order.shipping_address` is `undefined` (or `null`) at that point. When you then try to read `.city` off of it, JavaScript throws a `TypeError`.

**Why it works locally but not in production:** Your local test data almost certainly always includes a fully-populated `shipping_address` object. In production, some orders arrive without one (digital goods, local pickup, a partially-completed checkout, an API edge case, etc.), so `shipping_address` is missing.

## Fix

Use optional chaining (`?.`) so that if `shipping_address` is absent, `city` becomes `undefined` instead of throwing. The existing `|| "standard"` fallback then handles the undefined city gracefully.

<solution language="javascript" verdict="fix">
function getShippingZone(order) {
    const city = order.shipping_address?.city;
    const zones = {
        "New York": "east",
        "Los Angeles": "west",
        "Chicago": "central",
    };
    return zones[city] || "standard";
}
</solution>

The only change is `order.shipping_address.city` → `order.shipping_address?.city`. Everything else (the zone lookup and the `"standard"` fallback) remains exactly as before. If `shipping_address` is missing, `city` will be `undefined`, `zones[undefined]` will be `undefined`, and the function will return `"standard"` — no crash.

